                    coach_default:          `Move logged.`,
                    coach_r1_layer:         `<strong>That’s the right read.</strong> Value is migrating up to the energy-and-data layer — the OS, the tariff optimization, the predictive maintenance. The box is becoming a commodity endpoint. Now the only question that matters: what asset lets <em>you</em> capture that layer instead of the platform?`,
                    coach_r1_box:           `<strong>“A better, quieter box” is exactly the trap.</strong> Excellent engineering — but the platform that just launched doesn’t care how good your box is; it sits on top and takes the energy-and-data margin. You’ve optimized the layer where value is <em>leaving</em>. Where is value going, and what did you build there?`,
                    coach_r1_nowhere:       `<strong>“It’s a hype cycle” is how incumbents get commoditized.</strong> Two utilities have already bundled the rival OS. The boundaries between HVAC, energy retail and software are dissolving in real time — calling it hype just means you’ll react after the value has migrated and the layer is gone.`,
                    coach_r1_brand:         `<strong>Reputation alone won’t carry you into the new layer.</strong> Against a global platform with 50 million users, brand is necessary but not the platform for recapture. Your real, un-replicable asset is the 240,000 installed units and the installer relationships — name that or you’ll pivot on aspiration.`,
                    coach_r1_patents:       `<strong>Doubling down on thermal/mechanical patents fortifies the layer value is leaving.</strong> Those patents protect a box that is being commoditized into someone else’s endpoint. The defensible asset for the new layer is the installed base and the customer relationship — not more hardware IP.`,
                    coach_r1_explow:        `<strong>“Negligible exposure” understates the bleed.</strong> Analysts see the premium hardware margin compressing from 11% toward 6–7% within three years — that’s roughly <em>CHF 20–24M of EBIT a year</em>. Under-quantify the risk now and you’ll under-fund the response.`,
                    coach_r1_expignore:     `<strong>“React when margins fall” is reacting after the value has already migrated.</strong> By the time the 6–7% margin shows up in the P&L, the platform has the customer, the data and the recurring revenue. The founding-partner window closes in Round 3 — diagnosis is not optional.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You located the migrating value in the energy-and-data layer, named the installed base as the asset to reel it onto, and quantified the CHF 20–24M of EBIT at risk — and you’re treating the shock with real urgency. That is the platform from which a genuine reconfiguration is possible.`,
                    coach_r2_caas:          `<strong>Climate-as-a-service on the installed base is the modelled reconfiguration.</strong> You stopped selling a one-time box and started capturing recurring energy-and-data value on 240,000 units no entrant can replicate. Value-capture climbs, recurring revenue builds, and commoditization risk falls — because you’re competing on the layer where value now lives.`,
                    coach_r2_caasfast:      `<strong>Right model, too much throttle.</strong> Climate-as-a-service works, but flooring the ramp before the service and the installers are ready strains the transition and rattles the legacy P&L. Ease off — let the recurring base compound rather than forcing it in one year.`,
                    coach_r2_caasslow:      `<strong>Right model, barely moving.</strong> The platform’s founding-partner window is closing; a near-zero ramp means you arrive after the layer is captured. Climate-as-a-service only reels value in if you actually ramp the recurring base.`,
                    coach_r2_betterbox:     `<strong>Pouring the effort into a premium next-gen box is the defend-the-box error.</strong> It’s a more efficient endpoint inside someone else’s ecosystem. EBIT keeps eroding toward 6–7% and your value-capture of the new layer goes nowhere. You optimized the layer value is leaving.`,
                    coach_r2_software:      `<strong>“Helvetia the software company” — against a platform with 50 million users and 300 engineers, with what edge?</strong> You set aside the one asset that’s actually defensible — the 240,000 installed units — and pivoted on aspiration. Business-model innovation has to be anchored on a real asset, not a slide.`,
                    coach_r2_softwareanchored: `<strong>A software push, at least anchored on the base.</strong> Building on the 240,000 units is the right instinct — but a standalone-app framing still under-uses the recurring, service-and-maintenance value those units generate. Climate-as-a-service captures more of the migrating layer than an app alone.`,
                    coach_r2_bypass:        `<strong>Bypassing the 1,200 installers arms your own disruptor.</strong> They make their living fitting your boxes and they’re your distribution moat against the platform. Disintermediate them and you’ve turned your strongest ally into your loudest opponent — and weakened the ecosystem you’ll need in Round 3.`,
                    coach_r2_channelignore: `<strong>Launching the subscription without a word to the installers is a cannibalization landmine.</strong> The 1,200 independents will read climate-as-a-service as you competing with them for the customer. Unmanaged channel conflict can sink an elegant model — co-design it with them or it bites you later.`,
                    coach_r3_alliance:      `<strong>The utility-led open alliance is the strong play.</strong> You share the energy-and-data layer on open standards <em>while keeping the customer relationship and the installed-base data</em>. High value-capture, real ecosystem strength, and resilience if any single partner wobbles — exactly the posture that reels value in without handing away the customer.`,
                    coach_r3_coalition:     `<strong>A European manufacturer coalition gives you scale.</strong> Pooling installed bases and R&D is a credible counterweight to the platform, with decent control. It’s slower to mobilize than an alliance and the governance is heavier — but it keeps you out of the supplier’s lock-in and funds a shared layer.`,
                    coach_r3_supplier:      `<strong>Component supplier without reading the lock-in is Common Error 3.</strong> Fast and cheap, yes — but you just agreed the platform owns the customer, the data and the recurring revenue, and you’re back to a commoditized box at 6% margin with a platform tax on top. What value did you capture, and what did you hand away forever?`,
                    coach_r3_supplierfair:  `<strong>Supplier, but you contracted to keep the relationship.</strong> Negotiating that you retain the installed-base data and customer relationship softens the lock-in — though as a complementor you’re still inside someone else’s platform, with less control of the layer than an alliance would give you.`,
                    coach_r3_proprietary:   `<strong>Building the Helvetia OS is the boldest — and most resource-fragile — play.</strong> Full control and margin, but it’s a multi-year, CHF-heavy software race against a player spending ten times your capped budget. It only survives if you fund it seriously in Round 4 — and you still have three years before yours is ready.`,
                    coach_r3_concede:       `<strong>Conceding the layer on that utility’s grid hands the rival a beachhead.</strong> Accepting a dumb-endpoint role anywhere teaches the market the box is a commodity. A defection should trigger diversification, not surrender — resilience is the whole point of the ecosystem choice.`,
                    coach_r3_hope:          `<strong>“It won’t happen” is not a contingency.</strong> The brief tells you a key utility is threatening exclusivity — betting the model on one partner’s goodwill is exactly the disruption-resilience failure the round is testing. Diversify partners and bind the base directly before the defection lands.`,
                    coach_r3_lockinblind:   `<strong>“Whoever the platform decides” is lock-in blindness.</strong> Who owns the customer, the data and the recurring revenue <em>is</em> the value question. Speed to market is worth little if the speed is into someone else’s margin pool. Contract for the relationship or you’ve captured nothing.`,
                    coach_r4_reallocated:   `<strong>That’s a genuine reallocation.</strong> You funded the new model in the realistic software band and stayed inside the CHF 38M cap — which means you actually cut legacy hardware to pay for it, rather than writing a wish list. Now the value-capture and recurring revenue have the R&D behind them to be real.`,
                    coach_r4_protect:       `<strong>Reallocation funded and defended on the right ground.</strong> You stayed inside the cap, funded the migrating value, and led the board defense with <em>group EBIT</em> — proving the plan protects and grows the number the board actually judges, not a software narrative. That is the board-ready case.`,
                    coach_r4_paper:         `<strong>Your two R&D boxes sum to more than CHF 38M — that’s a wish list, not a reallocation.</strong> The board will not raise the cap. If you won’t cut a legacy hardware program to fund the software, the new model is unfunded. Which program dies? Until you answer that, you haven’t reallocated.`,
                    coach_r4_underfunded:   `<strong>You protected hardware and starved the new model.</strong> A few million of software R&D will not fund climate-as-a-service or an OS against a player spending ten times your budget. Genuine renewal means cutting legacy programs — a reallocation you won’t actually make is no reallocation at all.`,
                    coach_r4_overrotate:    `<strong>You over-rotated into software and starved the hardware that still earns the EBIT.</strong> The box business funds the transition; gut its R&D entirely and you erode the very group EBIT the board is judging. Reallocate decisively, but not recklessly — the realistic software need is around CHF 14M, not the whole budget.`,
                    coach_r4_narrative:     `<strong>Leading the board with a software vision invites the activist probe.</strong> They’ve seen the platform pitch deck before. With a 50-million-user rival, the only defense they’ll trust is the EBIT math: how does the reconfiguration protect group EBIT? Lead with the number, not the narrative.`,
                    coach_r4_reassure:      `<strong>Promising “nothing really changes” concedes the whole thesis.</strong> If the hardware business is safe and untouched, you haven’t reconfigured anything — and the board knows the 6–7% margin scenario is coming. Reassurance that denies the convergence is the least credible defense in the room.`,

                    sum_default:            `Move made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Located the migrating value, named the installed base, quantified the EBIT at risk.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Mis-read where value is migrating or which asset is defensible.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Climate-as-a-service on the base, with installers co-designing it.`,
